Workstream ID: omscs-path-explorer
Title: Gold Circuit: compare OMSCS AI specialization paths and elective choices
Status: IN_PROGRESS
Milestone: Publish an intuitive phone-ready degree-path review workbench
Dependencies: geojack-omscs, versioned-roadmaps
GitHub issue: https://github.com/Zanark/CareerOS/issues/13
Purpose: Replace a single prescribed-plan assumption with transparent choices:
  complete online AI specialization allocations, the broader free-elective
  catalogue, completed-course reuse and proposed calendars. Keep private
  planning assumptions separate from public source and saved career progress.

Tasks:
  [x] Review official AI menus, current courses, degree rules and return policies.
  [x] Implement current-menu and conservative source-comparison rule sets.
  [x] Enumerate complete specialization choices without double-counting roles.
  [x] Expose all current credit-course choices and the non-CS/CSE credit limit.
  [x] Add understandable editable choices, comparisons and semester proposals.
  [x] Correct operation naming to Gold Circuit and program naming to Georgia Tech.
  [x] Supply private local preview assumptions without public source defaults.
  [x] Preserve academic records, learning evidence, Atlas and frozen roadmaps.
  [x] Qualify constraints, actual browser behavior, privacy and responsive views.
  [x] Update focused documentation, evidence and this issue/Project mapping.
  [x] Clarify the phone-first selection steps and private draft starting basis.
  [x] Explain qualitative career/roadmap fit and the opportunity cost of subjects.
  [x] Provide a readable final shortlist for the owner's review notes.
  [x] Support draft-only return confirmation and an explicit starting subject.
  [x] Qualify the exact mobile candidate and public/private input boundary.
  [ ] Commit/push the public-safe candidate and monitor its exact Pages run.
  [ ] Verify actual hosted phone workflows and same-identity completion tracking.

Acceptance criteria:
  The interface distinguishes ten courses/thirty credits from ten credits.
    Completion, grade eligibility, free electives and specialization slots
    are distinct. Unknown grades never become verified GPA or degree approval.
  Current online menus, unused-core elective rules and unavailable campus
    entries are explicit. The older catalog disagreement and readmission
    catalog policy remain visible rather than silently resolved.
  Every structurally valid specialization allocation in the selected rule set
    is represented; role mappings and distinct course sets are counted separately.
    Free-elective possibilities are factorized, not falsely summed as globally
    unique complete degrees or materialized as millions of rendered cards.
  The complete current regular-course catalogue is browsable. Courses count
    once, seminars do not count and non-CS/CSE credits cannot exceed six.
  Reported prior courses affect private what-if budgets and proposed calendars,
    not stored academic or learning records. Planning grants no Atlas credit.
  Return-gap, foundational-grade and six-year credit-age checks are clearly
    conditional and source-backed. No readmission, seat or future offering
    is guaranteed; no academic account or application is accessed.
  Actual personal planning inputs stay outside public source, issues, shared
    context and production assets. Existing browser profiles are not automated.
  The approved published application, existing checkpoint identities, normal
    backups and encrypted journal remain intact. Private preview and testing
    use separate loopback processes/disposable data.
  The November19evening follow-up explicitly authorizes coherent commits,
    pushing and deploying the candidate for phone review. Keep human acceptance
    separate: the owner will inspect the actual published site afterward.
    No private planning input, transcript or real browser state is published.
  A new phone can enter prior completions in an explicitly private what-if
    draft without changing academic records. Guidance explains subject selection,
    direct/supporting/specialist career fit, roadmap links and a final shortlist.
    Do not invent numeric fit scores, university endorsements or assured outcomes.
  Return confirmation changes only the notice, never credits or saved records.
    A chosen first subject changes only ordering; if it is no longer a new
    selected subject, stop the calendar until deliberate replacement/reset.
    Preparation warnings remain visible without inventing prerequisite locks.

